# The Myth of the River Keepers

In the beginning, two spirits were chosen to guard the Great River that nourished all life. The River Spirit of the Mountains controlled the flow from above, and the River Spirit of the Valley shaped the waters below.

Each dawn, the Mountain Spirit received five drops of sacred rain. She could choose: release them into the river, or keep them for her own springs. When released, the drops multiplied threefold as they cascaded down—but only the Valley Spirit decided how much water would flow back upstream to replenish the mountain.

For many seasons, the Mountain Spirit held back her rain, fearing the Valley Spirit would keep everything. The Valley Spirit, receiving nothing, grew bitter and vowed to hoard any water that came.

The land withered.

One desperate dawn, the Mountain Spirit chose trust. She released all five drops. Fifteen drops reached the valley. The Valley Spirit, moved by this faith, returned ten drops upward, defying gravity itself through sheer reciprocity.

The pattern continued. Some days more flowed down, some days more returned up. Neither maximized their own gain, but both had abundance. The river swelled, and life flourished.

The spirits learned: trust multiplies what we release, but only reciprocity sustains the flow.
